{
 "manufacturer": "Unipi Technology",
 "manufacturer_slug": "unipi-technology",
 "model": "MM-OT101 Modbus / OpenTherm gateway",
 "model_slug": "mm-ot101",
 "short_model": "MM-OT101",
 "aliases": [
  "MM-OT101",
  "MM-OT1010",
  "Unipi OpenTherm gateway"
 ],
 "equipment_type": "Boiler gateway (OpenTherm to Modbus RTU)",
 "applies_to": "Unipi MM-OT101 DIN-rail gateway between Modbus RTU (RS-485) and OpenTherm/Plus boilers and thermostats, per its technical documentation.",
 "summary": "The MM-OT101 sits between an OpenTherm boiler and its thermostat (monitor mode) or replaces the thermostat (controller mode) and exposes the OpenTherm data on Modbus RTU. Each OpenTherm message ID maps straight to the register with the same number. This map covers the status flags, setpoints, temperatures, modulation, pressure, counters and remote parameters, the gateway's line settings and timing statistics, and the two mode coils.",
 "transport": [
  "RTU"
 ],
 "defaults": {
  "unit_id": 1,
  "baud": 19200,
  "parity": "Even",
  "data_bits": 8,
  "stop_bits": null,
  "tcp_port": null
 },
 "addressing": {
  "convention": "0-based",
  "note": "Register numbers equal OpenTherm message IDs (0-127) and are sent unchanged; register 0 (status flags) exists, so the numbering is 0-based. Gateway registers 1000+ and coils 0/1 follow the same rule."
 },
 "default_word_order": null,
 "firmware": null,
 "source": {
  "title": "Technical documentation - Communication gateway with Modbus and Opentherm MM-OT101",
  "doc_number": null,
  "revision_date": null,
  "url": "https://kb.unipi.technology/_media/files:products:extensions_gateyways:mm-ot101:mm-ot101-technical_manual_en.pdf",
  "pages": "p. 4 (modes, DIP switches), p. 5 (functions, mapping), pp. 5-9 (registers and coils)"
 },
 "notes": [
  "OpenTherm f8.8 values are signed 16-bit numbers divided by 256 (the manual's example: 1.3 is stored as 333).",
  "Registers that hold two 8-bit OpenTherm fields (flag8/u8, u8/u8, s8 pairs) are listed as one UINT16; split them into high and low byte yourself. The manual does not say which byte holds which field for every pair, so check against the OpenTherm specification.",
  "Left out: the 32-bit statistics counters (2004-2018 and 2104-2118), because the manual does not state their word order.",
  "In monitor mode, registers 0-127 hold the last value seen passing between boiler and thermostat; messages that never passed read 0. Unmapped registers and coils also read 0.",
  "The manual names separate read functions (a single-register read goes straight to the boiler in controller mode, a multi-register read returns the memory map) but gives no function-code numbers; registers are listed here as holding registers.",
  "Statistics units (bit times) are not stated.",
  "Register 0: the minimum-configuration table puts the master status flags (bit 0 CH enable, bit 1 DHW enable, bit 2 cooling enable, bit 3 OTC active, bit 4 CH2) in the high byte and the slave flags (bit 0 fault, 1 CH mode, 2 DHW mode, 3 flame, 4 cooling, 5 CH2 mode, 6 diagnostic) in the low byte, but the register map table prints the register as LB(flag8)/HB(flag8) for master and slave. Because the byte order is not settled, the bits are not mapped here; confirm on site."
 ],
